1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
The Aquarium

2
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:08,320
The coast is clear.

3
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:09,640
The humans have left.

4
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,519
Come on, let's go!

5
00:00:16,719 --> 00:00:19,960
Masiya, why are the fish looking so tired?

6
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:22,280
Because they're not getting enough air in there.

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:27,719
The water in the aquarium is dirty and it needs air, but the filter isn't working.

8
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:29,400
The filter?

9
00:00:29,399 --> 00:00:31,879
Yes, that device over there.

10
00:00:32,519 --> 00:00:36,399
These fish need our help! And if we don't do something right now, they could die!

11
00:00:36,519 --> 00:00:38,600
Right! First I'll fix that light,

12
00:00:38,759 --> 00:00:41,920
while you and Masiya go over there and see what is wrong with the filter.

13
00:00:42,399 --> 00:00:44,839
But I want to go and look at the filter too!

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:46,879
You're too small for this!

15
00:00:47,039 --> 00:00:48,320
Oh, whoa-whoa-whoa!

16
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:51,320
And you're a giant! I mean, you're like six feet tall, huh?

17
00:00:51,439 --> 00:00:54,280
That's enough arguing! Nolik, let's go!

18
00:00:59,399 --> 00:01:01,920
Well, let's check it!

19
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:03,600
Not working.

20
00:01:04,039 --> 00:01:05,280
Nolik, where are you?

21
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:06,400
I'm up here!

22
00:01:06,599 --> 00:01:08,200
What are you doing up there?

23
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:09,679
Nothing.

24
00:01:09,799 --> 00:01:10,840
Holding on.

25
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,439
We don't have time for that.

26
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:13,439
Get down!

27
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:15,759
We have to get this switch working!

28
00:01:20,879 --> 00:01:24,280
Masiya, what's the matter with the filter?

29
00:01:24,799 --> 00:01:29,280
Well, probably something's caught inside and it's stopping the motor from turning.

30
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:36,300
A filter is used to keep the aquarium water clean. A motor in a filter turns

31
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:41,560
the paddles and pumps water through a fine net or a sponge. The dirt in the

32
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:45,600
water gets trapped in there, and the cleaned water is put back into the

33
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:51,540
aquarium. Many filters not only clean the water but also add air to it, so there

34
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:57,180
will be more oxygen in the water. You see, even though fish live their lives in

35
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:00,100
water, they need oxygen just like all of us.

36
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:13,000
Hmm.

37
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:29,180
Tideesh!

38
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Way to go there!

39
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:40,000
Oh!

40
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:10,460
There are lots of ways for people to breathe under water.

41
00:03:10,460 --> 00:03:14,460
As an experiment, try putting an empty glass upside down in water

42
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:17,300
and you'll see that some of the air stays in there.

43
00:03:17,379 --> 00:03:20,260
That's the idea behind the ancient diving bell.

44
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:22,659
An empty bell was lowered under the water,

45
00:03:22,659 --> 00:03:26,060
and some of the air remained in there for the diver to breathe.

46
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:29,780
And about 200 years ago the first diving suits were invented.

47
00:03:29,939 --> 00:03:33,539
The diver got air from a hose that started above the water.

48
00:03:33,819 --> 00:03:37,259
This let the diver spend a long time under the water,

49
00:03:37,379 --> 00:03:40,579
and even walk around on the bottom, but just not too far.

50
00:03:40,859 --> 00:03:45,259
Later on, people learned to squeeze a lot of air inside of metal tanks.

51
00:03:45,419 --> 00:03:47,859
And that's when scuba diving started.

52
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:53,020
Scuba divers breathe the air stored in these tanks so they can swim freely,

53
00:03:53,020 --> 00:03:56,420
and even dive deep down below the water.

54
00:03:58,140 --> 00:03:59,500
Our work is done.

55
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:02,340
The light is on and the filter is working.

56
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:04,620
And the fish look so excited!

57
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:07,540
As if they're not fish but monsters!

58
00:04:07,540 --> 00:04:09,500
Thank goodness they're behind glass!

59
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:10,500
Papus!

60
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:13,700
Just hang on! We'll be right there to save you!

61
00:04:14,380 --> 00:04:16,699
But I don't even have my pack-o-mat!

62
00:04:16,699 --> 00:04:19,420
Look how they're chopping their teeth!

63
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:20,899
They must be so hungry!

64
00:04:20,899 --> 00:04:23,860
You're right! They're hungry! Nolik, come on!

65
00:04:43,539 --> 00:04:46,339
I can't believe those fish! They're so ungrateful!

66
00:04:46,339 --> 00:04:50,619
We went ahead and fixed their filter, and all they wanted to do was gobble us up!

67
00:04:50,739 --> 00:04:52,659
And I'm the one who saved you from them!

68
00:04:52,779 --> 00:04:54,699
I was the one watching what was going on.

69
00:05:00,339 --> 00:05:01,339
Oh, gee!

70
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:02,459
Hold it!

71
00:05:02,659 --> 00:05:04,779
Do you think giving her some, uh, food will help?

72
00:05:05,099 --> 00:05:07,659
As long as you're not thinking that food is me!

